  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nShowers and thunderstorms with localized downpours arrive  \nSunday afternoon into Monday, but antecedent drought conditions\nwill significantly limit the overall flood threat. A slow-\nmoving cold front approaching from southern Ontario will \ninteract with a closing upper-level low to pull a plume of \nmoisture rich air into the region. PWATs will climb to near 2 \ninches by Monday. Strong low level moisture convergence will \nsupport developing showers and thunderstorms, though the lack of\nstronger instability will largely limit any organized storms \nwith potential to reach severe thresholds. Showers and a few \nthunderstorms could lingering Monday night depending on the \nspeed of the cold frontal passage.\n\nThere is a potential for storms to move parallel to the slow-moving\nfront, leading to localized training, supporting locally high \nrainfall totals. The probabilistic models show the highest \nrainfall in the mountains, with <15% chance of 2 or more across\nthe area. This seems optimistic since rainfall rates and \ntraining could easily produce amounts 2-3. However, the \nmajority of the area can expect less than 0.5. The antecedent \ndry conditions will be a significant buffer against high impact \nflooding. Ensemble guidance shows a notable absence of small \nstream and river responses. Outside of sensitive urban centers, \nregional river basins will benefit from the rainfall. \n\nRegarding thunderstorm potential, there does seem to be at least\nsome isolated severe storm potential along and ahead of the\nfront on Monday. 12z NAM Nest soundings suggest that we could \nsee dewpoints in the upper 60s and lower 70s across southern New\nHampshire, yielding 1000 to 1500 J/kg of MLCAPE. This \ninstability will be collocated with effective shear around 40 \nknots and strong low level shear as well. The NAM Nest is \ncurrently the most bullish with it's parameter space but other \nlonger range CAMs do suggest the potential for at least a few \nstrong storms. Much will depend on how early day convection \nevolves and whether we get warm enough ahead of the front for\nupdrafts to become established. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBeyond Monday, a classic summer Bermuda High setup will take \nhold offshore for the remainder of the week. Persistent \nsouthwesterly flow will keep humid air locked in, with dew \npoints hanging in the 60s to low 70s and high temperatures in \nthe 80s. This pattern will favor diurnally driven chances for \nscattered downpours and weak afternoon thunderstorms\nparticularly later in the week. However most days will still \nfeature extended stretches of dry & humid conditions. Coastal \nareas can expect reprieves from the heat as onshore flow and/or \nsea breezes are likely most days, though it may be weak in \nnature.",
